WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.430
In this video, you create a Windows Server virtual machine from a prepared image, using the built-in Windows template so the Windows-specific settings are already in place.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:13.390
From the Virtual Machines page, select Create.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.360
Choose the namespace.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.000
Give the virtual machine a name.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.750
Select Use the virtual machine template.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:32.710
Choose the windows image template. It is meant for prepared, sysprepped Windows images, so no installer ISO is needed.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:41.620
The template sets the operating system type to Windows and turns on the USB tablet for accurate mouse input in the console.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.320
Keep the four virtual CPUs from the template.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.800
Lower the memory to eight gigabytes, enough for this test server.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.880
On the Volumes tab, the root disk from the template has no image yet.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.580
Remove this placeholder disk,

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:04.010
and add the image as a new disk instead. Select Add a Virtual Machine Image.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:09.720
In the Image list, search for the prepared Windows Server 2025 image,

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.090
and select it.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:17.520
Set the disk size to sixty-four gigabytes, the size the template suggests.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.520
Select Create.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:31.200
SUSE Virtualization copies the image into the new root disk and starts the virtual machine. It is now running.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:38.289
Windows Server is starting in its own virtual machine. Next, you add CPU and memory to a running virtual machine.
